  "description": "Roubaix Pro is a steel bike,\n\nbut it's not made from just any steel. The main tubes of the Roubaix Pro frame\n\nare Air-Hardened Reynolds 853 double-butted Cro-Moly steel. Famous Reynolds\n\n853 actually gets stronger when it's welded, unlike lesser tubes, which weaken\n\nfrom the torch's heat. Roubaix Pros come with a Fuji carbon fiber fork with\n\nalloy steerer, cool Ritchey Pro Road wheels, Shimano braking and shifting\n\nparts, Kore bar and Fuji Superlight stem, Michelin Axial Performance tires\n\nand Time MID clipless pedals. All good stuff. You may be trying to decide\n\nbetween a Roubaix Pro and an aluminum Roubaix. The steel Roubaix Pro'll feel\n\nmore resilient over road irregularities. It'll glide, not prance. There's virtually no difference\n\nin weight, but there is in ride and in appearance. The steel bike will look\n\nlike bikes have looked for decades. The aluminum bike will have fat tubes\n\nand look more \"today.\" Matter of taste. A steel Roubaix Pro will\n\nhave two chain rings, not three, 18 gears instead of 27. Ratios will be closer\n\nand the front derailleur will shift crisply. Eighteen gears is plenty, in\n\nall honesty, maybe all the gears your heart could desire.",
